//
// Tier escape: executing an instruction a tier does not implement
//

// This proposal stands alone. It is also the foundation for
// ideas/OpcodeExtensions.v3, which cannot work without it, but it is worth
// doing on its own terms and it depends on nothing that does not exist today.


// Motivation

// Wizard has three ways to execute an instruction -- the portable V3
// interpreter, the hand-written fast interpreter, and the single-pass compiler
// -- and they do not implement the same set. That is normal and healthy: a
// proposal lands in the interpreter first and is made fast later, and the
// README's feature matrix is largely a record of which tier has caught up.

// What is missing is a uniform answer to "this tier cannot do this
// instruction". Today there are three different answers:

//   SPC		bails out per function and falls back. Good.
//   V3 interpreter	a match arm; a missing one is a compile error. Fine.
//   fast-int		traps INVALID_OPCODE, which is wrong, and is also the
//			only thing it can do.

// Fast-int's dispatch tables are filled with a single default handler that
// traps, and slots are then overwritten for implemented opcodes. An opcode that
// is valid but unimplemented lands in a trap slot and the program dies with a
// diagnosis that is not true -- the opcode is not invalid, this tier just does
// not know it.

// Nor can fast-int fall back the way SPC does. On x86-64 the V3 interpreter is
// not compiled into the engine at all: build.sh selects TARGET_X86_64 *or*
// TARGET_V3, never both. There is no slower tier underneath to drop to.

// So an instruction fast-int does not implement has to be executed sideways --
// out to Virgil and back -- or not at all.


// The mechanism already exists

// It is how probes work. A probe stops fast-int at an arbitrary bytecode, calls
// into Virgil with access to the frame, and resumes:

//	def runtime_PROBE_instr(stack: X86_64Stack, func: WasmFunction, pc: int) -> Throwable {
//		var frame = TargetFrame(stack.rsp + Pointer.SIZE);
//		var ret = Instrumentation.fireLocalProbes(DynamicLoc(func, pc, frame));
//		if (ret != null) return ThrowResult.Unhandled.!(stack.throw(ret)).thrown;
//		return ret;
//	}

// Everything an escape needs is in those five lines: a frame to read and write,
// and a {Throwable} return that means "trap or throw" when non-null and
// "resume" when null. An escape handler is the same shape:

//	def runtime_ESCAPE(stack: X86_64Stack, func: WasmFunction, pc: int) -> Throwable {
//		var frame = TargetFrame(stack.rsp + Pointer.SIZE);
//		return Escapes.execute(func, pc, frame);
//	}

// Installing it is a one-line change in table construction. {genOpcodeHandlers}
// already computes exactly one default position and writes it into every slot
// of every page before overwriting the implemented ones. Slots for opcodes that
// are valid-but-unimplemented get the escape handler's address instead of the
// trap's. Every implemented slot is untouched.


// What is deliberately not needed

// The obvious way to build this would be a deopt: reconstruct interpreter state
// and hand the frame to another tier. Wizard has that, for SPC:

//	private def deoptToInterpreter() {
//		var map = SidetableMap.new(func);	// walks the entire function body
//		var stp = map[pc];
//		handle.writeInterpreterState(func, pc, stp);
//		handle.redirectToInterpreter();
//	}

// We do not want it and must not use it. {SidetableMap}'s constructor iterates
// the whole function to build a pc-to-sidetable map. That is acceptable for a
// deopt, which is rare; doing it once per executed instruction would be
// catastrophic, and the file already carries a "TODO: cache in FuncDecl?" on
// that line.

// The escape avoids it entirely, because an instruction that does not transfer
// control does not move the sidetable position. The handler advances the ip and
// leaves stp exactly as it found it. No map, no deopt, no tier switch, no
// reentry point -- just a call and a return, like a probe.

// This is the reason the proposal is small, and it is also the reason for the
// scope limit below.


// The one thing missing

// {FrameWriter} can write locals and frame variables but not operands:

//	def setLocal(i: int, v: Value)
//	def setFrameVar(i: int, v: Value)

// A handler that executes an instruction must consume its operands and produce
// its results, so it needs operand access. Reading already exists --
// {getOperand(i)} by depth, {numOperands()} from vsp minus vfp -- and so does
// the underlying write machinery, since locals and operands live in one value
// stack and {stack.overwriteValue} writes into it. What is absent is the API,
// not the representation.

// This is wanted independently. ideas/ProbeCapabilities.v3 already lists
// WRITE_OPERAND and WRITE_OPERANDS as capabilities a probe may use, spelled
// "DynamicLoc.getFrameAccessor().setOperand(#k)". A probe that can rewrite an
// operand is useful on its own, and it is the same API.


// Efficiency

// The governing constraint is that no instruction that runs today may get
// slower, and nothing here is on a hot path:

// - Dispatch tables are 256-entry pages, allocated and fully populated at
//   startup regardless. An escape fills slots that currently hold the trap
//   handler's address. No entry an implemented opcode uses changes, and
//   dispatch remains the same indirect jump through the same table. There is no
//   new branch and no new test.

// - The escape itself costs one indirect call plus a {TargetFrame}
//   construction, paid only by instructions that had no handler at all and
//   would otherwise have trapped. Nothing that previously ran pays it.

// - No change to SPC, whose bailout already exists and already costs nothing
//   when not taken.

// An escaping instruction is perhaps two orders of magnitude slower than a
// native handler. That is the right place for the cost -- it lands on the
// instruction nobody has written assembly for yet, which is precisely the
// instruction whose performance nobody is measuring.


// Scope

// This proposal covers instructions that do not transfer control. Branching
// instructions move the sidetable position, which is the one thing the escape
// deliberately does not touch; supporting them means reconstructing stp, which
// means the expensive machinery above. If we later want escaping control flow,
// it should be a separate step with its own justification, and the first move
// would be caching {SidetableMap} in {FuncDecl} as the existing TODO suggests.


// Hazards

// - Trapping and throwing from inside an escape. The {Throwable} return
//   protocol is right, but {stack.trap} and {stack.throw} were written against
//   fast-int's own frame assumptions, and the escape's frame is built at a
//   different point in the handler. This should be exercised early rather than
//   assumed; it is the most likely place for this proposal to be wrong.

// - Advancing the ip past immediates. The handler must know the encoded length
//   of the instruction it just executed. {BytecodeIterator} already computes
//   this, so the information exists, but the escape must use it rather than
//   re-deriving it, or the two will drift.

// - Operand writes and SPC. {FrameWriter.setLocal} today calls
//   {deoptToInterpreter} when the frame is an SPC frame, because SPC may be
//   holding that local in a register. Operand writes will need the same
//   treatment, and the interaction should be settled when the API is added
//   rather than after.


// Order of work

// 1. Add operand access to {FrameAccessor} and {FrameWriter}. Independently
//    useful for probes; no dependency on anything else here.
// 2. Add the escape runtime call and the ip-advance helper.
// 3. Fill valid-but-unimplemented dispatch slots with the escape rather than
//    the trap.
// 4. Prove it on an opcode that already exists: pick one fast-int implements,
//    remove its native handler behind a build flag, and check that the program
//    still produces identical results through the escape. That is a test that
//    can exist before any extension does.

// Step 4 is worth doing even if ideas/OpcodeExtensions.v3 never happens. It
// turns "fast-int does not support that proposal yet" from a wall into a
// slowdown, for every proposal, for free.
